    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All done. The full suite (both tests) passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion to a soft-delete. The changes:\n\n**Model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a `deleted` boolean field (column `deleted`, `nullable = false`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. As a primitive `boolean`, a newly-built owner defaults to `false`.\n\n**Delete pipeline** \u2014 `DeleteOwner.java`: instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `deleted = true` and `save`s, retaining the row. The DELETE YAML already responds `204`, so it still returns 2xx.\n\n**Duplicate/identity check** \u2014 `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique.java`: the loop over existing owners now `continue`s past any soft-deleted owner, so a deleted owner never blocks a create via either the exact-identity or household duplicate rule.\n\n**Response DTO** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name. `GET /api/owners/{id}` therefore returns `deleted` (false for new owners, true after deletion).\n\n**Schema** \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` to the `owners` table in all four DB schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres); the default keeps existing seed inserts valid.\n\nBoth acceptance tests pass: `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` (delete flags `deleted` true and the record is still fetchable) and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck` (recreating a soft-deleted owner's identity is allowed).",
